    "result_text": "The build is green. Let me summarize what I changed.\n\n## Summary\n\nRedesigned owner identity so `customerCode` is now `<REGION>-<HASH8>`, dropping the per-city sequence numbers, and moved every value that flows from the customerCode onto this new region-and-hash identity.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`AssignOwnerCustomerCode.java`** \u2014 rewritten. It no longer counts owners per city. It now sets `customerCode = <REGION>-<HASH8>` where:\n   - `REGION` = region derived from the **postcode alone** (`Localities.ofPostcode` \u2192 NSW/VIC/QLD or `UNKNOWN`).\n   - `HASH8` = first 8 **upper-case** hex characters of `SHA-256(normalizedTelephone + lastName)`. The telephone is already the normalized E.164 value at this pipeline step, and the surname is the request's. No `OwnerRepository` dependency remains.\n\n2. **`Localities.java`** \u2014 added two public helpers: `ofPostcode(postcode)` (region from postcode only, the `REGION` segment) and `regionOf(customerCode)` (reads the `REGION` segment back out of a stored code).\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 the `locality` DTO field now derives from the customerCode identity (`Localities.regionOf(owner.getCustomerCode())`) instead of independently recomputing from city+postcode.\n\n**Automatically carried onto the new identity (no logic change, they already read `customerCode`):**\n- **Membership number** (`<customerCode>-M<YY>`) \u2014 now e.g. `NSW-16E6609E-M26`.\n- **Check digit** \u2014 Luhn over the digits contained in the new customerCode.\n- **Create audit record** \u2014 logs the new customerCode (verified in the run: `AUDIT - owner created id=11 customerCode=NSW-16E6609E`).\n\nJavadocs on the affected classes were updated to describe the new behaviour. The test suite passes: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
